  "policy": {
    "seeding": "Seed every ceiling from at least 3 clean release-style runs on fresh hosted runners. Take a robust upper bound over the per-run median-of-round p95 values and multiply by a justified headroom factor. Never seed from a single run plus an arbitrary percentage.",
    "increases": "A budget increase requires a dedicated reviewed change recording the affected benchmark, old and new ceilings, the repeated raw measurements, the cause and user impact, the alternatives considered, and why optimization or rollback is not appropriate.",
    "decreases": "Ceilings may ratchet down to lock in a proven improvement once repeated measurements support the lower value.",
    "automation": "No task, script, or workflow in this repository may write this file. A breach never updates the budget: it blocks the release. A seeding or recalibration change may compute the entries with a one-off script from archived run reports, because every value is checkable afterwards: evidence names the runs and parseEntry re-derives each ceilingMs from observedUpperMs times headroom.",
    "environment": "Ceilings are only valid on the canonical target, Node version and runner image family. Drift in any of those three is a recalibration failure, not a comparison. runnerImageVersions records the exact image builds the ceilings were seeded on. A run on another build is reported as a diagnostic and does not stop the release, because GitHub rebuilds the image about every week and the project releases less often than that; the headroom over observedUpperMs covers the difference. A run that records no build at all stays a failure, because that is a defect in the measurement file. The CPU model cannot be pinned either: the report prints it in the environment line and it never stops a release, so spread the seeding runs across the models that appear."
  },
